          For most anglers who fish big lakes or open water, the Old Town Sportsman BigWater PDL 132 is the fishing kayak to buy: a 13 ft pedal boat that keeps both hands free. If you want to paddle and load the boat alone, the Perception Pescador Pro 12.0 is the better choice at 64 lb, and the Pelican Catch Mode 110 is the stable, low-cost way in. Choose by where you fish and how you will carry it, not by the longest spec sheet.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            A fishing kayak has to do three jobs at once. It has to be stable enough to cast, reel and net a fish without tipping, it has to carry you and your gear without riding low, and it has to be something you can actually get to the water. A boat that is stable but too heavy to load is a boat that stays in the garage, and a boat that is light but tippy is a boat you never stand up in.
          </p>

          <p>
            We built this list from manufacturer specifications, retailer listings and published reviews, and we note on each pick where the sources disagree. We did not lab-test these kayaks. Where a figure comes from a retailer or reviewer rather than the maker, we say so, and where we could not confirm a figure we leave it out rather than guess.
          </p>

          <p>
            We weighted four things above everything else: stability, measured mostly by width and hull shape; usable capacity, which is lower than the headline number; propulsion, meaning paddle, pedal or motor; and weight, because it decides whether you can launch alone. Price matters too, but fishing kayaks run from a few hundred dollars to several thousand, and the right answer depends far more on where you fish than on budget alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every pick is a sit-on-top. Sit-inside kayaks are lighter and warmer, but sit-on-tops are easier to re-enter after a fall, drain themselves through scupper holes and give you the open deck that fishing gear needs. If you mainly paddle rivers and want a boat for camping trips as well as fishing, our guide to the <a href="/best-canoes-for-river-paddling/">best canoes for river paddling</a> covers the other end of the paddlecraft spectrum.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="propulsion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Paddle, Pedal or Motor
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single biggest decision is how the kayak moves. A paddle kayak is the lightest, cheapest and simplest option. It is also the most tiring way to hold position in wind or current, because your hands are busy with the paddle at exactly the moment you want them on the rod. For ponds, small lakes and slow rivers, that trade is often worth it for the lower weight and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pedal kayak frees your hands. You drive the boat with your legs, which are stronger than your arms, and you can hold position, creep along a weed line or back away from a snag while still casting. Pedal drives come in two main styles: propeller drives, such as Old Town's PDL, which pedal forward and reverse like a bicycle crank, and fin drives, such as Hobie's MirageDrive, which push the boat with flapping fins that fold up when they hit something. Prop drives tend to reverse more easily; fin drives handle very shallow water better.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost of a pedal drive is weight and money. The pedal kayaks on this list weigh between roughly 85 and 110 lb rigged, against 54 to 67 lb for the paddle boats, and they cost several times as much. They also need a little more care: drives have to be rinsed, fins and props checked, and the drive well kept clear of weed and sand.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some anglers fit a small electric motor instead. Some kayaks are sold with a stern transom or motor mount, and several manufacturers sell kayak-specific motor kits. A motor covers distance without effort, but it adds the weight of a battery and may bring registration requirements depending on your state, so check local rules before you fit one. If you plan to run electronics or a motor, our explainer on <a href="/lifepo4-vs-lithium-ion-power-stations/">LiFePO4 versus lithium-ion batteries</a> covers why lithium iron phosphate has become the default for marine use.
          </p>
        </div>

