        <div id="stability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Stability, Width and Standing Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            Width is the quickest guide to stability, but it is not the whole story. The kayaks here run from 30.5 in wide on the Lifetime to 36 in on the Old Town PDL 106, and that range is roughly the difference between a boat you sit in and a boat you can stand in comfortably on calm water. Wider boats feel steadier at rest, which is called primary stability, but they are slower and push more water.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hull shape matters as much as width. Tunnel hulls, such as Pelican's, and double-U hulls, such as Old Town's, spread the boat's footprint out to the edges, which makes the deck feel planted when you shift your weight. Flatter, wider hulls are steady on still water but can feel less secure in chop, where a hull with more secondary stability, meaning it resists tipping further once it starts to lean, is more forgiving.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to stand and sight-cast, look for at least 33 to 34 in of width, a flat, grippy standing pad and a seat that lifts or folds out of the way. Practise standing in shallow, warm water first, and keep your feet apart and centred over the keel line. Standing raises your centre of gravity, so a boat that feels rock-steady seated can feel very different with you upright.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that stability changes with load. A kayak loaded close to its capacity sits lower, ships more water through the scuppers and becomes less stable, which is one reason to pay attention to usable capacity rather than the headline figure. A boat that is perfectly stable with a 180 lb paddler may feel noticeably livelier with a 250 lb paddler, a cooler and two tackle crates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Capacity: Total vs Usable
          </h2>

          <p>
            Capacity is the most misunderstood number on a kayak spec sheet. The headline figure is usually the maximum the hull can support before it becomes unsafe, and it often includes the weight of the boat's own pedal drive and seat. Old Town is unusually clear about this, listing both a total capacity and a usable capacity, which is the weight left for you, your gear and anything else once the assembled boat is accounted for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap is large. Old Town lists the BigWater PDL 132 at 425 lb total and 339 lb usable, and the Sportsman PDL 106 at 450 lb total and 343 lb usable. Other manufacturers publish only the maximum, so a useful rule of thumb from reviewers is to keep your loaded weight to around 70 to 80 percent of the rated maximum for a drier, better-handling ride.
          </p>

          <p>
            To work out what you need, add up your body weight in clothes and a PFD, then add your realistic gear: tackle crates, a cooler with ice, a fish finder and battery, an anchor, rods and water. Many anglers are surprised to find their gear weighs 40 to 70 lb. If that total sits above about 75 percent of a boat's rated maximum, move up a size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bigger paddlers should treat capacity as the first filter, not the last. The Lifetime Teton Angler 100's listed 275 lb maximum suits lighter anglers with modest gear, while the Vibe Shearwater 125's listed 475 lb maximum leaves room for a larger paddler and a full load. Choosing the right capacity matters more for comfort and safety than any feature on the deck.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="length" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Length, Tracking and Where You Fish
          </h2>

          <p>
            Length decides how well a kayak holds a straight line, which paddlers call tracking, and how quickly it turns. Shorter boats around 10 ft, such as the Pelican and Lifetime, turn easily, fit in a pickup bed and suit ponds and small, sheltered lakes. They wander more in wind and current, so you spend more effort correcting your course.
          </p>

          <p>
            Boats in the 12 to 13 ft range, such as the Perception Pescador Pro 12.0, Vibe Shearwater 125, Hobie Outback and Old Town BigWater, track better and cover distance with less effort. They handle wind and small chop more comfortably and are the better choice for larger lakes, tidal water and anywhere you expect to travel a mile or more to reach the fish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the boat to your water rather than to the biggest spec sheet. A 13 ft pedal kayak is overkill for a farm pond and awkward to launch from a steep bank, while a 10 ft paddle kayak is the wrong tool for a windy bay. If you fish a mix, the 12 ft class is the most versatile compromise.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rudders help longer boats in wind. Most pedal kayaks, including the Old Town PDL models, come with a rudder controlled by a hand lever, which lets you steer while pedalling. Paddle kayaks rely on your paddle strokes, and a few accept an aftermarket rudder kit if you find yourself fighting the wind on bigger water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="transport" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Transport, Launching and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weight is the spec that decides whether you fish. The paddle kayaks here weigh between about 54 and 67 lb, and the pedal boats between about 85 and 110 lb once the drive and seat are fitted. Most adults can lift one end of a 60 lb kayak onto a roof rack alone; very few can do the same with a 100 lb pedal boat, so plan your transport before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Options include a roof rack with a load-assist system or a side-loading roller, a pickup bed with a bed extender, and a small utility trailer, which is the easiest way to move a heavy pedal kayak. A kayak cart with solid wheels makes the walk from car to water far easier, especially on sand. Remove the pedal drive and seat before lifting, which takes several pounds off and protects the drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store kayaks out of direct sun, ideally on padded racks or hung from straps, with weight spread across the hull. Polyethylene hulls can deform if stored on a single narrow support for months, and long UV exposure fades and weakens plastic over time. A breathable cover helps if the boat lives outdoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to charge a fish finder or other electronics away from home, a small power station can top up batteries at a campsite or in the car; our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> compares capacity and charging options, and a <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel</a> can keep it charged on multi-day trips.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rigging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Rigging, Electronics and Storage on the Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fishing kayaks are platforms, and the deck layout matters as much as the hull. Look for accessory tracks, which let you mount rod holders, fish finders, cameras and anchor trolleys exactly where you want them; flush-mount rod holders behind the seat for trolling and storage; and an open rear tank well big enough for a tackle crate or cooler.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you plan to run a fish finder, check for a transducer scupper or a dedicated mounting point and a sealed battery compartment. Most kayak anglers use a small sealed battery or a lithium iron phosphate pack, mounted low and close to the centre line to keep the boat balanced. Route cables away from your feet and the pedal drive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seats deserve attention because you may sit for hours. Raised frame seats, common on pedal kayaks and premium paddle boats, keep you drier and make standing up easier, and most can be adjusted between a high casting position and a lower paddling position. Cheaper boats often use a padded seat that sits directly on the deck, which is wetter and harder on your back.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add gear gradually. It is tempting to rig a new kayak with everything at once, but every accessory adds weight and clutter. Fish the boat a few times, notice what you actually reach for, and mount those items first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Safety on the Water
          </h2>

          <p>
            The US Coast Guard requires a properly sized, Coast Guard-approved life jacket on board for each person in a kayak, and many states require it to be worn by children and during colder months. In practice, the safest choice is to wear it every time. Fishing-specific PFDs with low-profile flotation and tackle pockets are comfortable enough to wear all day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold water is the biggest risk most kayak anglers underestimate. A sudden fall into cold water can cause an involuntary gasp and loss of muscle control within minutes, long before hypothermia sets in. Dress for the water temperature rather than the air temperature, and practise re-entering your kayak from the water in warm, shallow conditions so you know you can do it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Make yourself visible. Kayaks sit low and are hard for powerboats to see, so a bright PFD, a visibility flag and, before dawn or after dusk, an all-round white light are worth having. Many states require a light for kayaks operating at night, so check local rules.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tell someone where you are going and when you expect to return, carry a whistle and a phone in a waterproof case, and keep an eye on the wind forecast. Wind is what turns a pleasant paddle into a hard slog back to the launch, and it usually builds through the afternoon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much to Spend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fishing kayaks fall into three rough price bands. Entry-level paddle kayaks, such as the Lifetime Teton Angler 100 and Pelican Catch Mode 110, sit at the bottom and are a sensible way to find out whether kayak fishing suits you. They are lighter and simpler but less stable, carry less and track less well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-range paddle kayaks, such as the Perception Pescador Pro 12.0 and Vibe Shearwater 125, add length, capacity and better seats and rigging. For many anglers this is the sweet spot: a boat good enough to keep for years without the weight or cost of a pedal drive, and in the Shearwater's case, a path to add pedals later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pedal kayaks, such as the Old Town Sportsman PDL 106 and BigWater PDL 132 and the Hobie Mirage Outback, cost several times as much. The money buys hands-free control, which makes the biggest difference for anglers who fish wind, current or open water, or who spend whole days on the water. If you mostly fish small, calm water for a few hours at a time, a good paddle kayak will serve you just as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for accessories too. A good PFD, a paddle worth using, a cart or rack and basic rigging can add several hundred dollars. It is better to buy a slightly cheaper kayak and the right safety gear than to stretch for a pedal boat and skip the essentials.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on the headline capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The maximum capacity often includes the boat's own drive and seat, and loading close to it makes a kayak ride low and wet. Add up your weight, clothing, PFD and realistic gear, then keep the total to about 70 to 80 percent of the listed maximum, or choose a boat that publishes a usable capacity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring how you will carry it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A pedal kayak can weigh more than 100 lb rigged, and many anglers cannot lift that onto a roof rack alone. Before you buy, decide whether you will car-top, use a truck bed or trailer it, and price a cart and a load-assist rack if needed. A boat you cannot launch alone is a boat you rarely use.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a pedal boat for a small pond</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pedal drives shine in wind, current and open water, but they add weight, cost and maintenance. If you fish small, calm water for a few hours at a time, a good 12 ft paddle kayak is lighter, cheaper and just as effective, and you can still fish hands-free with an anchor or stake-out pole.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Standing up before practising</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A kayak that feels steady seated can feel very different when you stand. Practise in warm, shallow water, keep your feet apart and centred, and use a standing pad and stand-assist strap if the boat has one. Never stand in moving current or chop until you are confident and wearing a PFD.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the PFD or wearing it loose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The US Coast Guard requires a properly sized life jacket on board, and wearing it is the single most effective safety step. Cold water can incapacitate a strong swimmer within minutes. Choose a fishing PFD comfortable enough to wear all day, and make sure it is snug enough not to ride up in the water.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

