        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best fishing kayak for beginners?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most beginners, a stable 10 to 12 ft paddle kayak is the best start. The Pelican Catch Mode 110 is a stable, low-cost option for ponds and small lakes, and the Perception Pescador Pro 12.0 is a light, well-tracking boat that most people can car-top alone. Both let you learn before you decide whether a pedal drive is worth the extra cost and weight.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are pedal fishing kayaks worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are worth it if you fish wind, current or open water, or spend long days on the water, because the pedal drive keeps both hands free for the rod. They cost several times as much as paddle kayaks and weigh far more, so on small, calm water a good paddle kayak with an anchor is often the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much weight can a fishing kayak hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Listed maximums on this list range from 275 lb on the Lifetime Teton Angler 100 to 475 lb on the Vibe Shearwater 125. The usable figure is lower: Old Town, for example, lists the BigWater PDL 132 at 425 lb total but 339 lb usable. Reviewers suggest staying within about 70 to 80 percent of the maximum for good handling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you stand up in a fishing kayak?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many fishing kayaks are designed for standing on calm water, especially those around 33 to 36 in wide with tunnel or double-U hulls and a standing pad. Practise in warm, shallow water first, keep your feet apart and centred, and sit down in wind, chop or current. Always wear your PFD when standing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What length fishing kayak should I get?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Around 10 ft suits ponds, small lakes and tight rivers and is easier to store and turn. Around 12 to 13 ft tracks better and handles wind and larger water more comfortably. If you fish a mix of water, a 12 ft kayak is the most versatile compromise.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How heavy are fishing kayaks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The paddle kayaks here are listed at roughly 54 to 67 lb, and the pedal kayaks at roughly 85 to 110 lb once the drive and seat are fitted. Remove the seat and drive before lifting, and use a cart for the walk to the water. Heavier boats are easiest to move on a trailer or in a truck bed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to register a fishing kayak?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most states do not require registration for a paddle or pedal kayak, but many do once you add an electric motor, and rules vary widely. Check your state's boating agency before fitting a motor. Whatever the registration rules, the Coast Guard requires a life jacket on board for each person.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
